          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-8607fd3b759f0ca3\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Julie’s written closing, “Je vous embrasse comme je vous aime,” embeds a strong avowal that she loves Marie. It presents continuing affectionate friendship as true. It does not substantially undertake a commitment, and its composed epistolary form provides no independent evidence of expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowal occurs within a conventional letter-closing formula and a comparative construction (“I embrace you as I love you”), but Marie’s reply explicitly interprets it as evidence that Julie still loves her.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"Julie writes the love avowal to Princess Marie as part of the affectionate closing of a signed letter.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The phrase expresses intimate but non-romantic friendship in a polished epistolary register. Its possible conventionality does not erase its avowal force: Marie immediately responds, “Vous m’aimez donc toujours,” treating the letter as confirmation of Julie’s enduring love. Nothing indicates that the words enact a distinct relational undertaking or escape deliberative control as a reflexive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Je vous embrasse comme je vous aime.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person present-tense wording presents Julie’s love for Marie as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The sentence appears after “Adieu” and other formal courtesies, immediately before the signature “Julie.”\",\"supports\":\"This identifies the event as a composed written letter-closing and supplies no positive evidence for expressive/reflexive E force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marie replies, “Vous m’aimez donc toujours, ma poétique Julie.”\",\"supports\":\"The addressee understands Julie’s wording as confirmation that Julie continues to love her, reinforcing the truth-conditional avowal reading.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The surrounding correspondence repeatedly uses the language of dear friendship, affection, absence, and people “chers” to the writers.\",\"supports\":\"The relevant love is framed as enduring affectionate friendship rather than as a promise or other relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the embedded avowal of continuing affection. The formal written delivery is contextual rather than a missing core-force dimension, and neither P, E, nor O is needed.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
